# 11B — Windows and CI builds

> **For the agent reading this:** Read `plans/11-distribution` first (the Control Panel, `src-tauri/`, how the app finds Node and the server, and its §8 fixes: `bundle.resources`, ad-hoc signing). 1.0.0 shipped as a macOS `.dmg` built by hand on Apple Silicon. This refinement adds **Windows** (a single portable `.exe` with no install, and an NSIS installer) and moves release builds to **GitHub Actions**, which also makes the Mac app **Universal** (Apple Silicon and Intel). Log progress in §7.

---

## 1. Goal

1. Orlando pushes a tag `v1.1.0`. GitHub Actions builds, on its own free runners (the repo is public), the Mac app for both architectures and the Windows builds, checks each one starts and serves the editor, and attaches them to a **draft** release.
2. A Windows user downloads `Orlablocks_1.1.0_x64_portable.exe` into Downloads and double-clicks it. SmartScreen warns once (**More info → Run anyway**). The Control Panel opens, the server starts with no console window, and **Open Editor in Browser** works. No install, no admin rights; it can run from a USB stick.
3. Someone who prefers an installer takes `Orlablocks_1.1.0_x64-setup.exe` instead (Start menu entry, uninstaller, installs WebView2 if it's missing).
4. A Mac user on Intel opens the same `.dmg` as one on Apple Silicon.

---

## 2. Scope

### In

- **Finding Node and the server per platform (`lib.rs`):** macOS as now (`Contents/Resources`); Windows next to the `.exe` (the installer's layout: `node.exe`, `dist\server\main.js`), else the portable payload. The zsh, Homebrew and nvm fallbacks become macOS and Linux only; Windows falls back to `where node`. Node starts with `CREATE_NO_WINDOW`, so no console window.
- **The portable payload (a `portable` cargo feature):** `build.rs` zips `dist/server`, `dist/web` and `src-tauri/binaries/node.exe` into the `.exe` (`include_bytes!`). At start the app unpacks it once to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\Orlablocks\runtime\<version>\` (into a temporary folder, renamed when complete, so an interrupted unpack is never used) and runs Node from there. A new version unpacks to its own folder.
- **WebView2 missing:** before opening the window, the app checks WebView2; without it, a native message says so and opens Microsoft's download page, instead of failing silently (the portable `.exe` can't install it; the installer does).
- **Staging Node on every platform:** `scripts/stage-node.mjs` (Node, not bash, since Tauri runs `beforeBuildCommand` through `cmd` on Windows) copies this machine's Node to `src-tauri/binaries/node` or `node.exe`; with `ORLA_UNIVERSAL_NODE=1` on macOS it downloads the official arm64 and x64 builds of the same Node version and joins them with `lipo`.
- **Platform config:** `tauri.macos.conf.json` (targets `app` and `dmg`, `node` as a resource, ad-hoc signing) and `tauri.windows.conf.json` (target `nsis`, `node.exe` as a resource, WebView2 bootstrapper), merged by Tauri over `tauri.conf.json`.
- **The workflow (`.github/workflows/release.yml`):** on a `v*` tag, and by hand (`workflow_dispatch`) to get test builds as artifacts. Jobs: `windows` (`windows-latest`: the installer, then the portable `.exe`, then a smoke test that starts it and asks `/api/health`), `macos` (`macos-latest`: `universal-apple-darwin`, a smoke test of the bundled server and `lipo -info` on its Node), and `release` (on a tag: a draft release with every file).

### Out

- **Code signing on Windows** (SmartScreen's warning) and **notarization on macOS** (Open Anyway): both need paid certificates; the README says how to get past them.
- **Windows on ARM**, and **Linux** builds.
- **Auto-update.**

